		<SimpleData name="page_url">https://www.dgll.nic.in/DGLL-light-house-location/about-jamnagar/ruvapari-lighthouse</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="rowlett">1922 (station established 1891). Active (?); focal plane 20 m (66 ft); white flash every 12 s. Square 1-story wood keeper's house with a pyramidal corrugated iron roof, mounted on Burmese teak pilings. Since 1994 the light has been shown from the top of a tall mast mounted on the roof. A DGLL photo is at right and Google has a satellite view; in both views the lighthouse appears high and dry on mud flats. The original lighthouse was destroyed by a tropical cyclone in 1920. The survival of this old-fashioned pile lighthouse is astonishing. The Indian Quarterly had an article by Ramesh Menon (no longer online) stating that the lighthouse has a resident keeper. The 16 teak straightpiles are abo</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="arlhs">IND-162</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="photo">https://www.dgll.nic.in/sites/default/files/2022-11/J-19.jpg</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="transcribed">1</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="ledger_issues">(3:Divergence 3° 26.18' not labelled horizontal or vertical,Tower type printed 'Grey hut o piles'; pedestal '40 feet wrought iron tapering mast fixed Screw Pile',Date of commissioning given only as year 1891)</SimpleData>
