		<SimpleData name="rowlett">1966 (station established 1836). Active; focal plane 39 m (118 ft); three white flashes every 15 s. 25 m (82 ft) round masonry tower with lantern and gallery. Lighthouse painted white; lantern roof painted red. Rahul Sharma has a 2022 photo, Shishir Desai has a 2016 photo, Manish Choudhary has a 2020 photo, and Google has a satellite view. Hazira, at the southern entrance to the Gulf of Khambhat, was one of the first British light stations in India; the 20 m (66 ft) round stone tower completed in 1836 was replaced by a 27 m (90 ft) round brick tower in 1867. Today Hazira is being developed as a major port facility to relieve pressure on the overcrowded piers of Mumbai. Located on a promontor</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="arlhs">IND-136</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="photo">https://www.dgll.nic.in/sites/default/files/2022-11/Hazira_Lighthouse.png</SimpleData>
