		<SimpleData name="page_url">https://www.dgll.nic.in/DGLL-light-house-location/about-portblair/keating-point-lighthouse-and-dgps-station</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="rowlett">1969. Active; focal plane 35 m (115 ft); white flash every 10 s. 37 m (121 ft) round cast iron tower with lantern and gallery, painted with red and white horizontal bands. This is a staffed station with crew quarters and other buildings. Ameya Deshpande's 2023 photo is at right, Sanjay Kumar has a 2018 closeup, Anup Patilhas a 2010 photo, and Google has a satellite view. This lighthouse marks the northern end of the Nicobar Islands group and therefore the southern side of the Ten Degree Channel, the passage between the Andamans and the Nicobars. The 2004 tsunami swept the point clean of vegetation and destroyed all the structures except the lighthouse, but the light station staff survived th</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="arlhs">AND-010</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="photo">https://www.dgll.nic.in/sites/default/files/2022-11/22.jpg</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="transcribed">1</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="ledger_issues">(4:Duration of Flash 0.25 Sec conflicts with characteristic fl 0.15,Effective beam intensity 1500 cds seems low for VRB-25 with 26 NM range; may refer to standby Sealite lantern,Optic R.P.M. blank,DGPS: Nautel 317 KHz 1000 Watts, RS IDs 438/439, Beacon ID 119; also NAVTEX Danphone 518/490 Khz 1000W)</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="equip_years">{ &quot;racon&quot;: 2005 }</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="equip_since">{ &quot;navtex&quot;: { &quot;year&quot;: 2015, &quot;src&quot;: &quot;network&quot; }, &quot;racon&quot;: { &quot;year&quot;: 2005, &quot;src&quot;: &quot;ledger&quot; }, &quot;ais&quot;: { &quot;year&quot;: 2012, &quot;src&quot;: &quot;network&quot; }, &quot;dgps&quot;: { &quot;year&quot;: 2003, &quot;src&quot;: &quot;network&quot; } }</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="tenders">(2:1898,1586)</SimpleData>
		<SimpleData name="photo_credit">DGLL</SimpleData>
	</SchemaData></ExtendedData>
